{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Educational Technology Evaluation","title":"Adoption Recommendation","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting adoption recommendation to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"05","section":"06","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation","cat531_supplement_adoption_recommendation","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/05/05"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_5_6_adoption_recommendation_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_5_6_adoption_recommendation_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_5_6_adoption_recommendation_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_5_6_adoption_recommendation_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":6,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# Adoption Recommendation\n\n![Adoption Recommendation textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/05-06-adoption-recommendation.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA recommendation is where an evaluation becomes a commitment, and the temptation is to flatten it into one word: adopt or reject. Real evidence is rarely that clean. A tool can show strong learning gains while its data agreement sits unsigned; the learning evidence argues for yes and the privacy evidence argues for not yet, and a bare verdict in either direction discards half of what the evaluation found. This section teaches the conditional recommendation: a statement that names scope, attaches conditions, and declares what would reverse it, so the decision matches the evidence instead of overrunning it.\n\nA conditional recommendation also protects the people downstream. An administrator who reads \"adopt\" launches a district rollout; an administrator who reads \"pilot grades 6 to 8 for one semester, conditional on a signed agreement by August 15, withdrawn if unmet\" knows exactly what was authorized and what was not. Precision in the recommendation is how a teacher keeps a decision honest after it leaves their hands.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Write a recommendation that matches the strength and the gaps of the evidence rather than rounding to adopt or reject.\n- Specify scope (who and what), step (pilot versus rollout), and condition (what must be true first).\n- Attach a reversal trigger that states what evidence or deadline would withdraw the recommendation.\n- Document why an unconditional adopt and a flat reject were both rejected in favor of a conditional path.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA committee evaluated an adaptive-math platform and found solid learning gains in a four-week trial, but the data-processing agreement is unsigned. The draft recommendation reads, in full, \"We recommend adopting the platform.\" One sentence, no scope, no conditions, no mention of the unsigned agreement that could expose student data.\n\nThe decision worth recording is not the verdict itself. It is whether the committee can write a recommendation that honors the strong learning evidence and the unresolved privacy gap at the same time, instead of letting one side of the evidence vanish.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA defensible recommendation has four parts, and a bare \"adopt\" has only a fraction of one:\n\n- **Scope** names exactly what is recommended and for whom: \"grades 6 to 8 math,\" not \"the platform.\" Scope prevents a narrow finding from authorizing a broad rollout.\n- **Step** names the size of the commitment: a one-semester pilot is a different act from a district-wide adoption, and the evidence usually supports the smaller step first.\n- **Condition** names what must be true before the step proceeds: \"a signed data-processing agreement must be in place before any student account is created.\" A condition converts an unresolved gap into a gate rather than a silence.\n- **Reversal trigger** names what would withdraw the recommendation: \"if the agreement is not signed by August 15, the recommendation lapses and the pilot does not start.\" A trigger makes the recommendation falsifiable and time-bound.\n\nThe reason both extremes fail is that they ignore part of the evidence. An **unconditional adopt** overstates the side the evidence does not support (here, privacy). A **flat reject** wastes the side it does support (here, learning). The conditional recommendation is not a hedge; it is the only statement that uses all of the evidence at once.\n\nThree course anchors keep the recommendation matched to evidence. **TPACK** anchors the scope to the specific learning the evidence actually covered (math, grades 6 to 8), not to the tool in general. **Critical edtech studies** insists that an unresolved risk becomes an explicit condition rather than a footnote. **AI and education datafication** is frequently the source of the condition itself, since data-handling gaps are the risks most often left unsigned.\n\nA defensible recommendation is built in order, narrowest commitment outward. Put the four parts in the sequence you would write them, from the claim that limits everything to the trigger that can undo it.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Scope: name exactly what and for whom, limited to what the evidence covered (grades 6 to 8 math)\",\"Step: name the size of the commitment (a one-semester pilot, not a district rollout)\",\"Condition: name what must be true first (a signed data-processing agreement before any student account exists)\",\"Reversal trigger: name what withdraws the recommendation (if unsigned by August 15, the pilot does not start)\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A committee found solid four-week learning gains for an adaptive-math platform, but the data-processing agreement is unsigned. The draft reads, in full, 'We recommend adopting the platform.' What is the core flaw?\" explanation=\"The bare verdict discards half the evidence. An unconditional 'adopt' overstates the privacy side the evidence does not support, while a flat 'reject' would waste the learning side it does. The conditional recommendation, with scope, step, condition, and reversal trigger, is the only statement that uses all the evidence at once.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It is fine; the learning gains justify a simple adopt.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"A bare verdict ignores the unsigned-agreement risk; it needs scope, a condition, and a reversal trigger.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It should be a flat reject because of the privacy gap.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nThe weak version below sounds plausible, but it does not yet give a reader enough evidence:\n\n> I made the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision clearer.\n\nThat claim is too thin because \"clearer\" does not identify the audience, the problem, or the evidence. A stronger claim would read:\n\n> I revised the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision so that the intended audience can see the decision, the constraint, and the evidence source. The revision is justified by **critical edtech studies**, and I can point to the exact part of the artifact that changed.\n\nThe improvement is not longer wording. It is a better trace: the reader can see the artifact, the constraint, and the exact revision that followed.\n\n### Worked Example: From Bare Verdict to Conditional Recommendation\n\nScenario: a committee evaluated an adaptive-math platform. Evidence: solid learning gains in a 4-week trial, but the data-processing agreement is unsigned.\n\n1. **First draft (before).** The recommendation reads: \"We recommend adopting the platform.\" One sentence, no scope, no conditions.\n2. **Matched the recommendation to the evidence.** Learning evidence is strong; privacy is unresolved. An unconditional \"adopt\" overstates the privacy side, and a flat \"reject\" wastes the learning evidence.\n3. **Wrote it conditionally.** Scope: grades 6 to 8 math only. Step: a one-semester pilot, not a district rollout. Condition: the signed data-processing agreement must be in place before any student account is created. Reversal trigger: if the agreement is not signed by August 15, the recommendation is withdrawn and the pilot does not start.\n4. **Annotated judgment call (after).** The revised recommendation reads: \"Recommend a one-semester grade 6 to 8 pilot, conditional on a signed data agreement by August 15; if unmet, no launch. We rejected an unconditional adopt because the privacy evidence did not support it, and rejected a flat reject because the learning evidence did.\"\n\nThe recommendation is defensible because a stakeholder can see exactly what is being recommended, on what conditions, and what would reverse it.\n\nNow write the committee's recommendation yourself, one decision at a time. The scenario starts from the bare draft and tests whether you match the recommendation to evidence that points two ways.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Adaptive-math platform: strong learning gains, unsigned data agreement\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"A four-week trial shows solid learning gains for grades 6 to 8 math, but the data-processing agreement is unsigned. The draft says \\\"We recommend adopting the platform.\\\" What do you write?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Keep \\\"adopt\\\"; the gains are real\",\"next\":\"adopt\",\"feedback\":\"You overstated the privacy side the evidence does not support.\"},{\"label\":\"Flip to \\\"reject\\\" because the agreement is unsigned\",\"next\":\"reject\",\"feedback\":\"You wasted the learning evidence the trial does support.\"},{\"label\":\"Write it conditionally with scope, step, condition, and reversal\",\"next\":\"cond\",\"feedback\":\"You build a recommendation that uses both sides of the evidence.\"}]},\"adopt\":{\"prompt\":\"An administrator reads \\\"adopt\\\" and launches a district-wide rollout with the agreement still unsigned. What went wrong?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The bare verdict widened the scope past the evidence and ignored the privacy gap\",\"next\":\"adopt_end\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. One word authorized far more than four weeks of grade 6 to 8 math could justify.\"}]},\"adopt_end\":{\"prompt\":\"Lesson: an unconditional adopt overstates the side the evidence does not cover. It silently widened a four-week math trial into a district rollout and waved past the unsigned agreement.\",\"feedback\":\"A bare verdict overruns the evidence.\",\"choices\":[]},\"reject\":{\"prompt\":\"You reject the tool. The math team protests that the trial showed real gains and asks why solid evidence was thrown out. What did the flat reject cost?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"It discarded the learning evidence the trial genuinely supported\",\"next\":\"cond\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Now use both sides: condition the recommendation instead of discarding half.\"}]},\"cond\":{\"prompt\":\"You set scope to grades 6 to 8 math and step to a one-semester pilot. The unsigned agreement is the unresolved risk. How do you handle it?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Mention it in passing, then recommend the pilot anyway\",\"next\":\"mention\",\"feedback\":\"You acknowledged the gap without gating it.\"},{\"label\":\"Write it as a condition: signed agreement before any student account is created\",\"next\":\"trigger\",\"feedback\":\"You convert the risk into a gate, not a footnote.\"}]},\"mention\":{\"prompt\":\"The pilot starts with the agreement still unsigned because nothing actually blocked it. What was missing from your ’mention’?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"A condition that gates the step plus a trigger that withdraws it\",\"next\":\"trigger\",\"feedback\":\"Add both so the risk actually controls the launch.\"}]},\"trigger\":{\"prompt\":\"Last part: the reversal trigger. What makes the recommendation falsifiable and time-bound?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"\\\"If the agreement is not signed by August 15, the recommendation lapses and the pilot does not start\\\"\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"Now the recommendation can be reversed by a future fact.\"}]},\"end\":{\"prompt\":\"Lesson: a one-semester grades 6 to 8 pilot, conditional on a signed agreement by August 15, withdrawn if unmet. You rejected the unconditional adopt (privacy not supported) and the flat reject (learning was supported). The recommendation uses every piece of the evidence.\",\"feedback\":\"This is the conditional recommendation the section teaches, falsifiable and dated.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nUse the strength of the learning evidence first, the unresolved risks second, and the size of the commitment last. A recommendation is defensible only when its scope, conditions, and reversal trigger match what the evidence does and does not support.\n\nBefore you submit a recommendation, test it against three questions:\n\n1. **Scope check:** Does the recommendation name exactly who and what, limited to what the evidence covered?\n2. **Condition check:** Is every unresolved risk written as a condition that must be met before the step proceeds, rather than mentioned and waived?\n3. **Reversal check:** Is there a stated trigger (a deadline, a missing signature, a failed metric) that would withdraw the recommendation?\n\nIf the recommendation cannot be reversed by any future fact, it is an opinion, not a defensible decision, and it should be rewritten with a trigger.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is treating a conditional recommendation as indecision; in fact it is more decisive than a bare verdict because it states precisely what will and will not happen and under what terms. The second is mentioning a risk in passing and then recommending adoption anyway, which acknowledges the gap without gating it. The third is writing a scope broader than the evidence, so a four-week math trial silently authorizes a year-long, all-subject rollout.\n\nA defensible recommendation states scope, condition, and reversal trigger explicitly, and records why both the unconditional adopt and the flat reject were rejected.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nStart from the bare draft, \"we recommend adopting the platform,\" and ask what evidence it rests on and what it ignores. The learning gains are real but cover only four weeks of grades 6 to 8 math; the privacy agreement is unsigned. The one-sentence verdict overstates the privacy readiness and silently widens the scope beyond what was tested.\n\nRebuild it part by part. Scope it to grades 6 to 8 math, the only learning the evidence covers. Set the step to a one-semester pilot, since a four-week trial cannot justify a district rollout. Attach the condition that the data-processing agreement be signed before any student account is created, converting the unresolved risk into a gate. Add the reversal trigger: if the signature is not in place by August 15, the recommendation lapses and the pilot does not begin.\n\nClose by recording the two rejected alternatives and why. Unconditional adopt was rejected because the privacy evidence did not support it; flat reject was rejected because the learning evidence did. A stakeholder reading the result sees a decision that uses every piece of the evidence and can be held to a date.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a four-slot note: scope, step, condition, reversal. For any recommendation you encounter, fill the slots; the empty ones are exactly where a bare verdict has overrun or wasted the evidence. A recommendation with no condition and no trigger should read as incomplete, not as confident.\n\nA strong reader of this section can answer four questions:\n\n1. What is the precise scope, and does it stay within what the evidence covered?\n2. Which unresolved risks are written as conditions rather than waived?\n3. What specific event would reverse the recommendation?\n4. Why were both the unconditional adopt and the flat reject rejected?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nFor this section, the recommendation is strong when scope, step, condition, and reversal trigger are all stated and the two extremes are explicitly rejected, so a mentor can see the decision matches the evidence on every side. Treat a one-word verdict as unfinished, regardless of how confident it sounds.\n\nA useful closing line would sound like this: \"I changed the recommendation to a one-semester grades 6 to 8 pilot, conditional on a signed data agreement by August 15, withdrawn if unmet. I kept out the suggestion to write a simple 'adopt' because it would have overstated the unresolved privacy evidence.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove to a tool with the opposite evidence profile: the data agreement is signed and airtight, but the learning evidence is a single anecdote from one teacher. The four parts still apply, but the condition now attaches to the learning gap, not the privacy gap: recommend a small pilot conditional on collecting comparison data this term, with a reversal trigger if the pilot shows no gain. The structure transfers even though the risk that drives the condition has flipped. Use the shift to confirm that you build the recommendation from where the evidence is weakest, not from a fixed template.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/05-06-adoption-recommendation-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"05.06\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **TPACK**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 05.06. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation\",\"cat531_supplement_adoption_recommendation\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_adoption_recommendation\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm the recommendation names scope and step, writes each unresolved risk as a condition, states a reversal trigger, and records why both the unconditional adopt and the flat reject were rejected.\n\n## References\n\n- TPACK. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- AI and education datafication. https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_5_6_adoption_recommendation_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_adoption_recommendation","stem":"An evaluation ends with: 'We recommend adopting the tool.' What turns this into a defensible adoption recommendation?","options":["Adding an exclamation point for emphasis.","Naming the scope, the conditions, the evidence behind the call, and what would reverse it.","Stating that the team liked the tool.","Listing every feature the tool offers."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A recommendation is defensible when it specifies who and what it covers, the conditions attached, the evidence behind it, and the signals that would trigger a reversal. A bare 'adopt' hides all of that.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_5_6_recommendation_is_verdict","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation","cat531_supplement_adoption_recommendation","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_5_6_adoption_recommendation_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_adoption_recommendation","stem":"The evidence is mixed: strong on learning gains, unresolved on data privacy. Which recommendation best matches that evidence?","options":["Recommend full adoption now and resolve privacy later.","Recommend a bounded pilot conditional on a signed data-privacy agreement, with a defined review point.","Recommend rejecting the tool because the evidence is not perfect.","Stay silent until the evidence is fully resolved."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A recommendation should be proportional to the evidence. Mixed evidence supports a conditional, bounded step (a pilot with a privacy condition and a review date), not an all-or-nothing call.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_5_6_adopt_or_reject_only","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation","cat531_supplement_adoption_recommendation"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_5_6_adoption_recommendation_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"You ask an AI tool to write your adoption recommendation. It produces a confident 'adopt' paragraph. What is the responsible use?","options":["Sign and submit it because it reads professionally.","Use it as a draft, then make sure the recommendation reflects your evidence, your conditions, and a decision you can stand behind to stakeholders.","Ask the AI to also approve the budget.","Adopt whatever the AI recommends to save time."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The teacher or committee, not the AI, is accountable to students and stakeholders for the recommendation. An AI draft must be checked against the actual evidence and conditions before it is owned.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_5_6_ai_owns_recommendation","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning","cat531_supplement_adoption_recommendation"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_5_6_adoption_recommendation_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"A committee adopts a tool on your recommendation, but a key privacy condition you stated is never met. What should your recommendation have made explicit?","options":["Nothing; once adopted, the recommendation is finished.","A trigger: if the stated condition is not met by a set date, the recommendation no longer stands and the tool is paused.","That the committee, not you, is responsible for everything.","A note that the tool was popular."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A defensible recommendation builds in accountability: it states the conditions and the consequence if they go unmet, so an unmet condition reopens the decision rather than being quietly ignored.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_5_6_recommendation_is_final","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision","cat531_supplement_adoption_recommendation"]}]}}